        text: " for a few months realized this was not going to work for me. And then it moves on to more modern style things, like Duelingo, Rosetta Stone, Himsler, I even use Memorize and built up over 16 million points, trying to memorize all these words using fancy, nomotics, and digital flashcards. And that worked a slightly better, but by the end of it, I was not fluent in Chinese by any stretch of the imagination. I started off like this guy, I came to China, I'm gonna do Chinese in a year, I said. And after a year and at least 1,000 hours, I was not only not fluent, I was getting laughed at by all of my Chinese friends. I realized that I'm not this gifted language learner at all. In fact, for the first time in my life, to be honest with you, I had really tried at something educational and I had failed. I never really did well at school, but that's because I never tried, but this time I really tried. And I was doing a job that I didn't like at all. I was an English teacher. And I realized that I was approaching 30, and I had no career, and all my friends were becoming surgeons and investment bankers. There's the curse of going to a reasonably good high school. And I was an English teacher. Not that there's anything wrong with English teaching, but there is when you hate it. So I was doing really badly in life at that moment. Until one day, I stumbled across a very special YouTube video. It was by a guy called Dr. Stephen Craschen, who is one of the most renowned linguistic researchers. And in this video, he said, we acquire language in one way and only one way by understanding messages. He talked about the importance of comprehensible input, reading, listening to, and watching the content of the language you want to learn. And if you do that enough, you just naturally acquire it. You just become able to magically speak it just like you could feel native language. And also, I saw this video, a light bulb went off, and I realized I'm trying to learn language based on what I learned in school about how to learn languages. But I did four years of high school French, and my French sucks. In fact, my Spanish is way better than my French, even though I never learned it in school. Do you know why? Because I went on holiday to Spain every year for like five years as a kid. So I stopped basing my learning on my own rubbish instincts about language learning, which was skewed based on this school mindset. And I started looking at what the professional language learners, the polyglots of the world, were doing to learn languages. And guess what? They were all focused around comprehensible input. Sure, some of you recognize some of the people in this picture. These are all polyglots. People that speak multiple languages. And while they have differing methodologies, of course, they all are big proponents of listening and reading and watching, consuming the content of the language you want to learn. It's essentially like this. You put comprehensible Chinese into your brain somehow. And then eventually you can speak fluent Chinese. I mean, you won't be a perfect speaker. You still have to hone that part of your skill set. But you can naturally speak really well if you input enough. And up until this point, I've been saying, I'm not going to learn those stupid squiggles. That's an actual quote from me, by the way, referring to the Chinese characters. And I'm not going to learn those stupid squiggles, but I'm going to do Chinese in a year. But now I realize that that was completely wrong. And I had to learn how to read pronto. So the cool thing about reading is that it is the most comprehensible form of input, because you can just take your time. It's very visual. And you can take as much time as you want to understand what the message is that you're trying to consume. And that's why, as adults, we can really use reading as like our number one hack for the language. A hack, by the way, that children don't have access to it because they can't really read very well. So reading gives us a huge advantage because we can get that comprehensible input earlier. Problem is, though, with Chinese, of course, there's not an alphabet, where we're just 26 letters. You actually need to memorize 3,000 of these random squiggles to be able to comprehend most of what you read in Chinese. And unfortunately, the best ways that I found of learning characters were basically just writing the characters over and over again. This is what Chinese kids have to do in school for like 10 years. I wasn't going to do that. But I also wasn't going to give up either. So I started looking at more smart ways to memorize Chinese characters. And then I stumbled across another YouTube video. This video, I recommend you guys go and watch it at some point. Here's by a guy called Joshua Four. This guy is a journalist who was investigating these memory athletes, these memory champions, that can memorize decks of cards and digits of pi, and things like that. And he actually realized that anyone can do these techniques. It is a series of techniques. These guys aren't like savants, it's photographic memories. They actually just use a technique that everyone can learn. And he learned it. And within a year, this guy, Joshua, he became a memory champion himself from zero using these techniques. And he wrote a book about it called Moon Walking with Einstein. And I used this technique to learn 3,000 Chinese characters in six months, which is about 45 minutes to an hour a day. And then just learn how to read them. I learned how to write them and pronounce them with the correct tone as well. I mean, I mastered these characters in like just a few months. This is about when I met Phil. And I shared with him the character technique. He shared with me the idea of immersion, spending hours a day just listening to podcasts in Chinese, watching TV in Chinese, reading books in Chinese. And his me finally realizing the power of comprehensible input. And after learning all those characters, Phil and I both immersed in Chinese for about six months. And then one day we realized we could understand and speak fluent Chinese almost like magic. So we went from failing. Essentially, I was considering quitting. Phil was just bored out of his mind in university and not really getting anywhere. And then we went in 500 hours or the space of 12 months, just an hour or two a day, to fluency and passing the HSK6. And that is the highest level Chinese exam at the time, which should take four years of full time"
